{"toolCallId":"call_02660809fd7d417c867531dc1363424f","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"#!/usr/bin/env node\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// MODULE: Repair Derived\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n'use strict';\n\n// Validation failures split into two kinds. Some are facts the repository\n// already knows and the document merely records wrongly: where a packet sits on\n// disk, what level it declares, whether its generated metadata still matches\n// its sources. Those can be recomputed and are what this repairs. The rest are\n// records of work someone did — evidence, verification results, decisions —\n// which cannot be derived from anything and must be written by whoever did the\n// work. Repairing only the first kind is the whole point: a tool that filled in\n// the second would make the gate green by making the packet lie.\n//\n// Dry by default; --apply writes. Everything --apply will write is named by the\n// dry run first, so the report is a faithful preview rather than a summary of\n// some of it. Idempotent, so a second run is a no-op.\n//\n// Exit: 0 = nothing left that this tool can repair; 1 = repairable work found\n// (dry run); 2 = something failed — a rejected argument, a packet whose report\n// could not be read, or a repair that did not land.\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 1. IMPORTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nconst { execFile } = require('node:child_process');\nconst crypto = require('node:crypto');\nconst fs = require('node:fs');\nconst os = require('node:os');\nconst path = require('node:path');\nconst { promisify } = require('node:util');\n\nconst run = promisify(execFile);\n\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\n// 2. CONSTANTS\n// ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────\nconst REPO = process.cwd();\nconst VALIDATE = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/spec/validate.sh';\nconst BACKFILL = '.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/runtime/cli/graph/backfill-graph-metadata.ts';\nconst TSX_LOADER = './.skilled/skills/system-spec-kit/node_modules/tsx/dist/loader.mjs';\n\n// Each packet is validated by its own process and nothing is shared between\n// them, so the wall-clock cost is a scheduling problem rather than a real one.\n// Serially this walk takes hours, which is long enough that nobody runs it.\n//\n// Nearly all of a packet's cost is the validator's own rule subprocesses, so a\n// worker spends most of its life waiting on children rather than computing, and\n// the pool is worth pushing past the core count. Measured over a 181-packet\n// tree on 18 cores: 12 workers 57.7s, 16 → 49.1s, 24 → 45.9s, 32 → 45.6s. The\n// curve is flat past 24 and every worker holds a shell plus its node children,\n// so that is where the cap sits.\nconst WORKERS = Math.max(4, Math.min(24, Math.ceil(os.cpus().length * 1.5)));\n\n// A wedged validator would otherwise hold a worker — and so the whole run —\n// open forever. A packet takes about two seconds; five minutes is a hang.\nconst CHILD = { cwd: REPO, encoding: 'utf8', maxBuffer: 64 * 1024 * 1024, timeout: 5 * 60 * 1000 };\n\n// Rules this tool can settle from repository state alone. Anything absent from\n// this list is reported and left alone, however mechanical it may look.\n//\n// The validator registry already classifies every rule, and this list is not\n// simply its structural category. Two deliberate differences, recorded here so\n// the next reader does not have to guess whether they were intended:\n//\n//   GRAPH_METADATA_SHAPE is structural and included, because the re-derive\n//   rewrites the very file it makes assertions about.\n//\n//   SPEC_DOC_INTEGRITY is classified as an authored-template rule and included\n//   anyway, because one of its two failure modes is a recorded location that\n//   has gone stale — recomputable — while the other is a reference to a file\n//   that no longer exists, which is not. Only the first is repaired; a packet\n//   failing solely on the second is reported as beyond this tool rather than\n//   passing silently.\nconst DERIVABLE = new Set([\n  'DESCRIPTION_SHAPE',\n  'GENERATED_METADATA_INTEGRITY',\n  'GENERATED_METADATA_DRIFT',\n  'GRAPH_METADATA_SHAPE',\n"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/cwd":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree","terminal_exit":{"terminal_id":"d9ed7d","exit_code":0,"signal":null},"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
{"toolCallId":"call_0dd95604c73d43efbc35173faae0452a","status":"completed","content":[{"type":"content","content":{"type":"text","text":"FILE_EXISTS | error | - | Validates required spec documents for the declared documentation level.\nPLACEHOLDER_FILLED | error | - | Rejects unfilled template placeholders in authored spec documents.\nCOMMENT_HYGIENE_MARKER | error | - | Rejects ephemeral finding markers inside HTML comments in authored spec documents.\nSCAFFOLD_NEVER_TOUCHED | error | - | Rejects scaffold-signature markers in required docs when spec.md claims Complete status.\nSTATUS_CROSS_DOC_CONSISTENCY | error | - | Advisory-by-default check that spec.md and implementation-summary.md status fields classify to the same delivery state.\nLEVEL_DECLARED | info | - | Reports whether the documentation level was declared or inferred.\nAC_COVERAGE | info | - | Default-on advisory (non-blocking) scan for acceptance-criteria traceability coverage; set SPECKIT_AC_COVERAGE=false to opt out.\nAC_CLOSURE | error | - | Gates packet closure on acceptance-criteria.md at Levels 2, 3 and 3+; unmet criteria block a completion claim and a waiver must cite an ADR that exists. Packets created before the cutoff stay advisory.\nANCHORS_VALID | error | - | Checks anchors in each spec doc whose template defines them, ignoring fenced code; a phase parent is exempt, and research/research.md and review/review-report.md are skipped as free-form workflow documents. Errors: a doc with no anchors, an anchor opened twice, never closed, closed but never opened, or closed more times than it is opened. Nesting is also an error: an anchor opened inside another, closed before it is opened, or closed while another is still open, though markers quoted in inline code are ignored by the nesting check; an adr-NNN anchor may hold adr-NNN-* anchors. Template order is not checked.\nFRONTMATTER_MEMORY_BLOCK | error | - | Validates canonical _memory continuity frontmatter blocks.\nMERGE_LEGALITY | error | - | Validates generated merge payloads against target anchor shape.\nSPEC_DOC_SUFFICIENCY | error | - | Checks implementation-summary sufficiency after save/write flows.\nCROSS_ANCHOR_CONTAMINATION | error | - | Detects route/category mismatches before generated content is merged.\nPOST_SAVE_FINGERPRINT | error | - | Verifies post-save fingerprints for generated writes.\nTOC_POLICY | error | - | Enforces table-of-contents placement policy.\nAI_PROTOCOLS | error | - | Validates AI execution protocol sections in high-governance specs.\nCOMPLEXITY_MATCH | error | - | Checks declared level against content complexity signals.\nFOLDER_NAMING | error | - | Validates numbered spec-folder naming conventions.\nFRONTMATTER_VALID | error | - | Validates YAML frontmatter structure and required semantic values.\nFRONTMATTER_VALUES | warn | - | Warns when a packet document's contextType or importance_tier is outside the shared list in sk-doc/sk-create-frontmatter/assets/frontmatter-values.json. Aliases are legal.\nSOURCE_TAGS | warn | - | Warns when a path:line citation inside a [SOURCE: ...] tag in a packet's research or review artifacts names a gone file, a moved file or a line past the end, using sk-doc's citation resolver. Packets created on or before the cutoff are skipped. A pass means the path and line exist, not that they support the claim.\nGREP_CONVENTION | error | - | Checks every markdown document in a spec folder against the greppable-corpus convention: frontmatter variant, trigger phrase quality, anchor grammar and naming.\nLEVEL_MATCH | error | - | Checks required files match the declared documentation level.\nGRAPH_METADATA_PRESENT | warn | - | Checks generated description and graph metadata are present.\nGRAPH_METADATA_CHILD_DRIFT | warn | - | Flags graph-metadata.json children_ids missing on-disk phase children the writer would add (advisory unless SPECKIT_CHILD_DRIFT_ENFORCE=true).\nGRAPH_METADATA_CHILD_IDENTITY | error | - | Flags graph-metadata.json children_ids entries whose leading path identity is not the packet's own current identity (stale rename residue the refresh writer now prunes).\nGRAPH_METADATA_SHAPE | warn | - | Validates graph-metadata.json shape: required packet/derived blocks, last_active_child_id pointer integrity for phase parents.\nMETADATA_DISK_PATH_CONSISTENCY | error | - | Advisory-by-default check that description.json, graph-metadata.json, and continuity ids match the real on-disk spec-folder path.\nDESCRIPTION_SHAPE | warn | - | Validates description.json shape: required name/description/level keys present and well-typed.\nNORMALIZER_LINT | error | - | Strict-only lint for duplicated runtime normalizer helpers.\nSPEC_DOC_INTEGRITY | error | - | Verifies inline markdown references resolve within spec docs.\nTEMPLATE_SOURCE | error | - | Checks SPECKIT_TEMPLATE_SOURCE provenance markers.\nCANONICAL_SAVE_ROOT_SPEC_REQUIRED | error | - | Ensures canonical saves include a root spec reference.\nCANONICAL_SAVE_SOURCE_DOCS_REQUIRED | error | - | Ensures canonical saves include source document references.\nCANONICAL_SAVE_LINEAGE_REQUIRED | error | - | Ensures canonical save lineage metadata is present.\nCANONICAL_SAVE_PACKET_IDENTITY_NORMALIZED | error | - | Checks canonical save packet identity normalization.\nCANONICAL_SAVE_DESCRIPTION_GRAPH_FRESHNESS | error | - | Checks generated description/graph freshness for canonical saves.\nCONTINUITY_FRESHNESS | error | strict | Strict-mode freshness check for continuity frontmatter. The rule reports warn by default and escalates to fail under SPECKIT_COMPLETION_FRESHNESS_ENFORCE; the ceiling here has to allow that escalation, since a warn ceiling would silently cap it.\nGENERATED_METADATA_INTEGRITY | error | strict | Validates description.json and graph-metadata.json through the shared schemas plus path-prefix and status-enum invariants, error under strict behind a grandfather report mode.\nGENERATED_METADATA_DRIFT | error | strict | Re-derives a folder and reports drift between the stored description/causal_summary and the current docs, read-only, error under strict only when the drift-gate flag is on and otherwise grandfather report mode.\nLINKS_VALID | warn | - | Scans the system-spec-kit skill's own markdown for wikilinks whose target file does not exist. The target is always this skill, never the validated packet.\n"}}],"_meta":{"cognition.ai/cwd":"/private/tmp/claude-501/-Users-michelkerkmeester-MEGA-Development-Code-Environment-Public/fcd93cd7-b3f2-45e6-b976-87fabe792df3/scratchpad/speed-loop/w904a7ae601/tree","terminal_exit":{"terminal_id":"7be582","exit_code":0,"signal":null},"cognition.ai/inferenceToolName":"exec"}}
